@section Toolchain

@noindent
This edition is written against OCaml 5.5.1 and Dune 3.24, pinned exactly in
@file{ocaml/dune-project}. ``Pinned exactly'' means the version numbers in that
file are not minimums: @code{opam switch} for this project must resolve to
5.5.1, and every listing in this book was run against that compiler and no
other. Two OCaml releases apart can print a float differently or accept a
syntax the other rejects, so a pinned switch is what makes ``type this in and
get this'' a promise the book can keep.

@subsubheading Creating and using the switch

An opam switch is a self-contained installation of the compiler and its
libraries, the same idea as a Python virtualenv or a Node version manager's
pinned version. Create one for this project and select it once:

@example
opam switch create 5.5.1
opam install . --deps-only --with-test --with-dev-setup
@end example

@noindent
Every command in this book that invokes the compiler, Dune, or a formatter is
prefixed with @code{opam exec --switch 5.5.1 --} in this edition's own
tooling, so the right switch is used even when the shell's default switch is
something else. Interactively, @code{eval $(opam env)} inside a shell already
attached to the 5.5.1 switch drops the prefix; both spellings reach the same
compiler.

@subsubheading The interpreter

Type @code{ocaml} at a shell prompt and a read-eval-print loop starts:

@example
$ ocaml
        OCaml version 5.5.1

# 1 + 1;;
- : int = 2
@end example

@noindent
The @code{#} is the interpreter's prompt, not something to type. Every phrase
ends with @code{;;}, and the interpreter replies with the phrase's inferred
type and its value, in the form @code{- : @var{type} = @var{value}}. A
top-level @code{let} names the value instead of discarding it, and the reply
changes shape to name it:

@example
# let square x = x * x;;
val square : int -> int = <fun>
# square 21;;
- : int = 441
@end example

This book's transcripts show that same form throughout: a phrase after the
toplevel's @code{#} prompt, then the reply @code{- : @var{type} =
@var{value}}.  The plain @code{ocaml} toplevel that ships with every OCaml
installation prints these exchanges exactly as written.  The everyday choice
in the OCaml community is @code{utop}, a toplevel that adds history,
completion, and colored output to the same language and the same replies;
its prompt is decorated, but the words of each reply match this book's.
@code{utop} is not a dependency of this project---the chapters build and test
against what @file{modern-sicp.opam} pins---so install it separately if you
want its comforts:

@example
$ opam install utop
@end example

@noindent
Whichever toplevel you follow along in, the language is the one this book
teaches and the replies are the ones printed here.

@subsubheading Building this edition

Dune is this edition's build system: it compiles every chapter's library,
runs its tests, and can reformat its source. Three commands recur through
every chapter of this book, run from the @file{ocaml/} directory:

@example
opam exec --switch 5.5.1 -- dune build
opam exec --switch 5.5.1 -- dune runtest
opam exec --switch 5.5.1 -- dune fmt
@end example

@noindent
@code{dune build} compiles every library and executable and fails on the
first type error or unused-value warning; this edition treats warnings as
errors, so a build that finishes has no loose ends. @code{dune runtest} runs
every chapter's tests, including the small executable behind each section of
this chapter that reproduces its listings and checks them against the values
printed here. @code{dune fmt} reformats every source file to one consistent
style (JaneStreet, 90 columns) and is safe to run at any time; it changes
whitespace only, never behavior.

@subsubheading How a chapter's directory is laid out

Chapter 0's own supporting code sits under @file{ocaml/ch0/}, and every later
chapter repeats the same shape:

@table @file
@item lib/
The chapter's running examples: one module per section (@code{sec_0_1},
@code{sec_0_2}, and so on), plus a small executable per section that
replays its listings and checks each result. Running
@code{dune runtest} runs every one of these executables; a failure there
means this book's own printed output is wrong, and the build refuses to
pass until it is fixed.
@item exercises/
One module per exercise (@code{ex_0_01} through @code{ex_0_05} in this
chapter), each exposing the signature the exercise asks for with a body
that raises @code{Sicp_common.Pending.Pending_solution}. The @code{scaffold}
alias (@code{dune build @@scaffold}) runs every stub and confirms each one
still raises that exception, i.e., is still unsolved.
@item solutions/
The reference solution for each exercise, under the same module names,
built against the same signatures as @file{exercises/}, plus a short
@file{.md} file per exercise explaining the reasoning. A chapter's test
suite links against @file{solutions/}, never @file{exercises/}, so an
unsolved exercise never breaks the build.
@end table

@noindent
A test earns its place in this book only by checking an actual result. The
convention that makes that checkable is @code{Sicp_ch0.Replay.expect}: it
prints a value and exits with a nonzero status if that value differs from
what the section's executable claims. Section 0.10 covers testing itself in
detail, once the pieces this chapter needs (pattern matching, exceptions,
records) are in place to explain how it works.

With the switch pinned, the three commands above green, and the directory
layout in view, the rest of this chapter introduces exactly the language this
book uses, section by section.
